Installing p9share

From a checkout:    ./autogen.sh && ./configure && make && make check && sudo make install
From a tarball:     ./configure && make && make check && sudo make install
As a package:       make deb && sudo apt install ./debbuild/p9share_*_*.deb

Install it on both machines.  Then:

  the machine with the files (as yourself):    p9share export DIR HOST [NAME]
  the machine that shows them:                 sudo p9share mount USER NAME DIR

  (USER is the account on the receiving machine that the exporter logs in to.  `p9share export` prints the line to run there.)

The p9share service is enabled by the package and started at boot (sudo systemctl start p9share to start it now).
From `make install` it is installed but you enable it yourself:  sudo systemctl enable --now p9share
On WSL2 (no systemd):   sudo p9share wsl-boot      then restart WSL once (wsl.exe --shutdown), or run  sudo p9share boot --detach

ssh must work without a password from the machine with the files to the one that shows them:   ssh HOST true

`make check` needs no root: it tests the configuration, a real daud over 9P, and the exporter (with a stand-in for ssh).
The mount itself needs root and the 9p kernel module, so it is not part of it: try it on two machines as above.
